coverage={'systems':[M,B,T,H],'basis':'WHO-HAEM5 nomenclature, with broad study families and selected molecular distinctions retained. WHO authors’ 2022 classification papers are the baseline; the public draft structure can contain older terminology.','merged':['AML differentiation and genetic subsets combined except APL; MDS subtypes combined.','B-ALL and T-ALL molecular subsets nested inside broad lineage entries.','Marginal zone lymphomas and selected EBV-associated LBCL categories combined, with aliases and internal distinctions.','Post-cytotoxic, germline-predisposition and immune-dysregulation settings are qualifiers on underlying disease, not separate repeated mutation lists.','Histologic Hodgkin, follicular, ALCL and TFH variants grouped where suitable.'],'excluded':['CHIP/CCUS, MGUS, monoclonal B-cell lymphocytosis, in situ lymphoid lesions and transient abnormal myelopoiesis are not separate malignant entries.','Reactive histiocytoses, Castleman disease, lymphomatoid papulosis and indolent cutaneous lymphoproliferative disorders excluded.','Uncertain/provisional NK-lymphoblastic categories and nonmalignant mature plasmacytoid proliferations are not independently catalogued.'],'limitations':['Broad-family coverage is not a certified exhaustive WHO code crosswalk.','Rare lymphomas have limited driver evidence; several entries deliberately emphasize virus or lack of a defining alteration.','LCH and ECD are included as clonal histiocytic neoplasms; clinical aggressiveness varies widely.','Somatic mutations shared with clonal hematopoiesis do not establish a malignancy without diagnostic context.']}
